1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:08,000
Imagine your body as a city of billions of cells, each one holding up tiny protein fragments on its surface

2
00:00:08,000 --> 00:00:15,000
so the immune system can inspect what's being made inside. When something goes wrong — a virus, or a cancer mutation — a T-cell comes along and kills the cell.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:24,000
That inspection process is run by MHC class I proteins. They bind to short peptides from inside the cell and present them on the surface.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Predicting which peptides a given MHC will present is one of the most important computational problems in immunology.

5
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:45,000
It's the foundation of neoantigen vaccine design and cancer immunotherapy.

6
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:55,000
In our paper, we approach this as a machine-learning problem: sequence ranking under a context key.

7
00:00:55,000 --> 00:01:02,000
Ten-thousand candidate peptides, one HLA allele, rank them.

8
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
We built DeepNeo-CL — a compact transformer, 35 million parameters, trained on the official NetMHCpan-4.1 folds.

9
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:20,000
But the paper isn't really about the model. It's about how we evaluate.

10
00:01:20,000 --> 00:01:35,000
Here's the problem. The most-cited predictor in this field, NetMHCpan, trains on peptide-HLA pairs that end up in almost every public benchmark.

11
00:01:35,000 --> 00:01:50,000
If you don't remove the overlap, your test set is partly a train set, and your reported gains aren't real.

12
00:01:50,000 --> 00:02:05,000
On top of that, every paper reports results across dozens of HLA alleles — and treats each one as an independent win, so pure chance gives you lots of "significant" results.

13
00:02:05,000 --> 00:02:18,000
So we did two things. We removed every shared training pair before scoring, and applied Holm's multiple-testing correction across the family of allele-level comparisons.

14
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
Here's what happens under that honest protocol.

15
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:40,000
On the TransPHLA benchmark — 118-thousand clean rows after decontamination — our compact PLM matches NetMHCpan-4.1 at the fused score we deploy.

16
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:45,000
Not better, not worse. Matched.

17
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:58,000
On the IEDB weekly leaderboard — 14 predictors scored over 46 weeks — we sit mid-field for binding affinity,

18
00:02:58,000 --> 00:03:05,000
and we lead the three-predictor eluted-ligand board.

19
00:03:05,000 --> 00:03:15,000
NetMHCpan still beats us on the pure binding-affinity head. That's in Table 1. We don't hide it.

20
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:27,000
And here's the most interesting failure. We tried a second-stage rescoring head, fit by cross-validation on the evaluation benchmark itself.

21
00:03:27,000 --> 00:03:32,000
In-sample AUROC jumped to 0.862.

22
00:03:32,000 --> 00:03:37,000
Under a fair, allele-held-out protocol, it dropped to 0.442 — below chance.

23
00:03:37,000 --> 00:03:43,000
The classic transductive-overfitting trap. We withdrew the component.

24
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:55,000
The takeaway: a compact protein language model can rank peptide-MHC pairs competitively against a strong industrial baseline — when the comparison is leakage-controlled.

25
00:03:55,000 --> 00:04:02,000
Code and benchmark results at github.com/KevinSimple/deepneo-pub. Paper under review at MDPI Information.

26
00:04:02,000 --> 00:04:05,000
Thanks for watching.
